# A rank that left cannot rejoin the ranks still running (issue #129): the NCCL communicator they formed, which rank
# 0's CUDA graphs captured, takes no new process in its place. A worker started again alone never paired (rank 0's
# rendezvous store stays open, and a new rank 1 read the old session's NCCL id from it), while rank 0 kept its
# weights, answered /v1/models and hung every request. Now every rank ends (exit code 1) when the ranks it needs are
# gone, so a supervisor (FOREGROUND=1 in a systemd unit, a container restart policy) starts them again and they form
# a new set: rank 0 when another rank shows no sign of life for TENSORFOLD_PEER_TIMEOUT_S seconds (default 120; 0:
# never) or starts again, a rank past 0 when rank 0's store has not answered for as long (at three Sparks the worker
# that did not restart otherwise waits in a collective forever), except after rank 0 marked a clean stop: a flush that
# rank still writes then finishes. Each rank calls the store every 2 s from a daemon thread on a connection of its own
# (the engine's waits on the idle doorbell for up to an hour); rank 0 reads the beats and the count a rank adds as it
# reaches the rendezvous, drops the NCCL id once every rank holds it (a rank starting again waits for a new rank 0's),
# and marks a clean stop as cuda/http.py's serve begins it (SIGTERM, Ctrl-C). CPU check: tools/peer_watch_check.py.

diff --git tensorfold/cuda/comm.py tensorfold/cuda/comm.py
--- tensorfold/cuda/comm.py
+++ tensorfold/cuda/comm.py
